In a DNA molecule, adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T) and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). Therefore, if the DNA molecule is 20 percent adenine, it will also be 20 percent thymine, because the percentages of adenine and thymine must add up to 40 percent (since A pairs with T). This means the combined percentages of guanine and cytosine are 60 percent (since G pairs with C and A and T together represent 40 percent of the total). Since guanine and cytosine are equally represented, each makes up 30 percent of the molecule. Since there are 180 base pairs, there are 180 x 0.30 = 54 cytosine nucleotides. The answer is not listed, but if we could, it would be 54.
