The issue that confounds me is, one side has proven that UV-C light can be replicated inline in metal ducts and can effectively sterilize live bioaerosols. On the other side, I routinely come across cutting edge research, consensus driven by peer-reviewed journals, demonstrating outside the scope of this topic that UV-C light cannot penetrate dirty & grimy, heavily populated metal ducts effectively, and if it could, the operator should never be present during exposure due to the harmful effects of UV-C, and have below it a plea for us to invest more in cleaning bioaerosols. When we talk about those bioaerosols in the field, we are not usually talking about single puddle-sized blobs of microorganisms, but we’re referring to high concentrations (from aerosolized dead cell bursts and otherwise) that are constantly generated by the microbes living simply - those same microbes that have been continuously assimilated and attenuated. The unseen danger that a consortium of otherwise benign organisms poses, when combined with crowd density, is what actually promotes disease.

